messages:
maya: FYI I hit a confusing telemetry/logging race in the Node sampleapp while onboarding. If requests include X-Request-Id some log lines switch timestamp format to small floats and the level sometimes flips to debug. Repro below, anyone seen this?

kevin: that sounds like a monkeypatch/order issue. paste logger + otel init?

maya: Boot order: config -> init logger -> init OTEL auto-instrumentation -> express middleware. Snippet:```js
// logger.js
const pino = require('pino');
module.exports = pino({ level: process.env.LOG_LEVEL || 'info', timestamp: pino.stdTimeFunctions.isoTime });

// app.js
const logger = require('./logger');
require('./telemetry')(); // opentelemetry init
app.use((req,res,next)=>{ req.log = logger.child({ reqId: req.headers['x-request-id'] }); next(); });
```

kevin: can you paste raw log lines showing tiny float + level flip?

maya: sample from an ab run:```
maya-service GET /api/foo 200 - 0.000234ms debug { reqId: 'abcd-1234' }
maya-service GET /api/foo 200 - 1617973.1234 info { reqId: 'efgh-5678' }
```
Tiny vs large float, and level differs.

sam: do you use pino pretty or multi-streams? any console patching? otel sometimes wraps console.

maya: no explicit console touches. Removing OTEL init removes the bug. Ordering (logger before/after otel) didn't change. Removing the logger.child assignment also removes it.

alex: child loggers inherit serializers lazily; if otel monkeypatches time or console bindings it could cause sporadic child behavior.

kevin: try a reduced repro without express: create parent pino logger, register otel instrumentation, spawn many child loggers and log.

maya: did that:```js
const pino = require('pino');
const logger = pino({ level: 'info', timestamp: pino.stdTimeFunctions.isoTime });
const child = (id)=> logger.child({ reqId: id });
// register OTEL instrumentations here
for (let i=0;i<200;i++){ child('id-'+i).info('hello'); }
```
With OTEL, ~15-25% of lines show tiny float + debug. Without OTEL, all uniform.

sam: two suspicions: 1) OTEL instrumentation patches Date/console/time helpers, 2) pino child inherits a binding lazily and race leads to mixed behaviors.

alex: workaround ideas: force timestamp fn on child (not public API), or avoid child altogether and pass reqId as structured field when calling logger methods.

kevin: short-term: set LOG_LEVEL=info globally and avoid creating child loggers in middleware; call logger.info({ reqId }) instead. Also try pinning/downgrading OTEL packages.

maya: I tried wrapping logger.child to always create a new pino instance with the same timestamp fn; reduced frequency but hacky.

sam: please open a minimal repro issue (include Node, pino, otel versions) and tag infra/eng-ml.

ci-bot: Created INF-2026: "pino child logger + opentelemetry instrumentation causes inconsistent timestamp/level" https://tracker/redwood/INF-2026

maya: PR with repro + notes: https://github.com/redwood-inference/sampleapp/pull/88 . Suggestions for sampleapp fix: (A) avoid child loggers in middleware, (B) create independent per-request loggers, or (C) pin OTEL until upstream fixes.

alex: vote A + add doc explaining the race and recommended logging recipe for onboarding. Add a smoke check to onboarding script.

kevin: I'll help add a short devex doc and link INF-2026. :thumbsup:

maya: thanks — updating PR with docs and workaround now.
